You have an interface `interface HasId { id: string }` and you write the following generic function:

```ts
function resetRecord<T extends HasId>(item: T): T {
  return { id: "default-id" };
}
```

Why does TypeScript report a type error on `return { id: "default-id" }`?

You want to write a utility function `pluck<T, K extends keyof T>(record: T, key: K): T[K]`. A teammate writes:

```ts
function pluck<T, K>(record: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return record[key];
}
```

What compilation error does TypeScript raise for this signature?

You are writing a generic store interface with a constrained type parameter and decide to provide a default type argument:

```ts
interface Store<T extends { id: string } = { id: string; name: string }> {
  items: T[];
}
```

Which rule governs the default type assigned to `T`?
